      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Contributions to Black Hole Physics</strong><br>\nIn addition to gravitational waves, Thorne has made substantial contributions to the study of black holes. He has explored their nature and the way they interact with their surroundings, proposing new ways of thinking about these mysterious objects. Thorne's investigations have helped to explain how black holes can emit radiation, a phenomenon now known as Hawking radiation after physicist Stephen Hawking.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Thorne and the Science of Time Travel</strong><br>\nThorne also ventured into the more speculative realms of physics, including the possibility of time travel. He proposed a theory involving wormholes, which are theoretical passages through space-time that could create shortcuts for long journeys across the universe. Thorne's work examined whether the laws of physics would permit such phenomena, inspiring both scientists and science fiction writers alike.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Thorne and Popular Culture</strong><br>\nNotably, Thorne has also been involved in bridging the gap between science and popular culture. He served as a scientific consultant and executive producer for the film \"Interstellar,\" which presented complex scientific concepts, such as black holes and relativistic time dilation, to the public. His involvement ensured that the film's depiction of these concepts was as accurate as possible within the confines of Hollywood storytelling.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
