          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Honors and Awards</strong><br>\nThroughout his career, Thorne has received numerous honors for his contributions to science. These include the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2017, which he shared with Rainer Weiss and Barry C. Barish for their decisive contributions to the LIGO detector and the observation of gravitational waves. His achievements have established him as a leading figure in the field of theoretical physics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Kip Thorne the Educator</strong><br>\nThorne is recognized not only for his research but also for his role as an educator. He has taught generations of students, emphasizing the importance of theoretical physics. Through his textbooks and public lectures, Thorne has worked to make complex scientific ideas accessible to students and enthusiasts alike.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Future Work</strong><br>\nAs of now, Thorne continues to inspire through his ongoing work and advocacy for science. His current research interests extend beyond his previous work on gravitational waves and black holes, aiming to further unravel the mysteries of the universe. Thorne's legacy in theoretical physics is marked by his passion for uncovering the underlying principles that govern our vast cosmos.</p>\n"
              },
